DECIDED by the maintainer 2026-08-28. This supersedes what #74 originally
said (forward the two timeouts into the eleven body-hash methods) and also
reverses part of #72.

THE SHAPE

  $docker->containers->using(read_timeout => 5)->create(Image => 'alpine')
  $docker->images->using(connect_timeout => 2, read_timeout => 30)->pull(...)

->using returns a CLONE of the resource class carrying the values, which it
passes to _request on every call made through it. The client attribute stays
the rule; ->using is the exception for a run of calls.

AND THE PER-METHOD OPTIONS COME OUT AGAIN

All 72 of them, added in #72. Three ways to say the same thing is one too
many; the maintainer's words: "das verwirrt nur, das machts zu kompliziert".
After this there are two, and they sit at different levels:

  API::Docker->new(read_timeout => 30)          the rule, for a client
  $docker->containers->using(read_timeout => 5) the exception, for a run

That also dissolves this ticket's original problem rather than solving it: if
create and the other ten read no options at all, their argument list is
unambiguously the request body again, and neither the HashRef form nor a
delete out of the body hash is needed.

WHY NOT ->with()

Measured, not assumed: Moo exports `with` as the role-composition keyword,
and every resource class calls `with 'API::Docker::Role::Filters'`. Defining
`sub with` shadows it -- perl warns "Subroutine with redefined" and the role
is then silently NOT composed. A stealth bug. `using` collides with nothing.

WHAT THE BUILD HAS TO GET RIGHT

  - the clone must carry the same client, which the resource classes hold as
    a weak_ref -- a clone that takes a strong reference, or that outlives the
    client, is a leak or a crash
  - ->using must not mutate the original: two clones from one resource class
    must not see each other's values
  - the entity classes hold the client differently; decide whether
    $container->using(...)->logs is in scope, and say why either way
  - where the shared code lives: API::Docker::Role::Filters is the existing
    precedent for a role every resource class consumes

Related: #72 (which added what this removes), #61 and #59 (the timeouts
themselves), #70.
